; tpm-keyring-unlock: let the unseal helper reach the TPM from a GDM login.
;
; GDM runs its PAM stacks in the SELinux domain xdm_t, and so does everything
; they start - pam_tpm_keyring_authtok's helper and the tpm2_* tools it runs
; included. Fedora's policy does not let xdm_t open the TPM, so without this
; rule every fingerprint login and unlock fails to unseal, with "Failed to
; open specified TCTI device file /dev/tpmrm0: Permission denied" (GitHub
; issue #23). A typed password never needs the helper.
;
; This is all of xdm_t, not only the helper: every process GDM runs as root
; may open the TPM with it, and file permissions keep everything else out
; (/dev/tpmrm0 is root:tss 0660). README.md, "SELinux", says what that gives
; up and why no narrower domain was written instead.
;
; open, read and write are all the helper uses, on its fast path and on the
; slow one that recreates the primary: measured on Fedora 44, enforcing, and
; again with xdm_t made permissive, which logged no other denial.
;
; install.sh loads this with `semodule -i`; uninstall.sh offers
; `semodule -r tpm_keyring_unlock`.
(allow xdm_t tpm_device_t (chr_file (open read write)))
